    "id": "other-faiths-judgement",
    "q": "What about sincere people of other faiths or none, and what about judgement and hell in this view?",
    "short": "Christians hold several serious views: exclusivism, inclusivism, hopeful universalism, convinced universalism, conditional immortality and the traditional view of eternal punishment. The relational gospel insists that Jesus is the only Saviour and that his judgement is the judgement of love. Many in this stream hope that all will finally be reconciled, but they hold that hope in different ways.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "What many of us were taught",
        "body": [
          "Many grew up with a simple answer: anyone who has not consciously trusted Jesus before death goes to hell, forever, in conscious torment. That includes the devout Muslim neighbour, the kind atheist grandfather, and the person in a remote village who never heard the name of Jesus. Some found this unbearable and quietly stopped believing it; others kept it but felt unable to look at it closely.",
          "Others were taught the reverse in popular culture: all religions are basically the same, and sincerity is what counts. Neither answer takes Jesus or the world’s religions seriously enough."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The main Christian views, fairly",
        "body": [
          "<strong>Exclusivism</strong> (many evangelicals): salvation comes only through conscious faith in Christ in this life (John 14:6; Acts 4:12; Rom 10:14). The Lausanne Covenant denies that general revelation in nature “can save” and rejects the idea “that Christ speaks equally through all religions”. <strong>Inclusivism</strong> (Catholic teaching, many others): salvation is only through Christ, but Christ can save some who do not know his name. The Catholic Catechism says those who, “through no fault of their own, do not know the Gospel… but who nevertheless seek God with a sincere heart… may achieve eternal salvation” (CCC 847), while insisting the Church must still evangelise (CCC 848).",
          "<strong>Hopeful universalism</strong>: we may hope and pray that all will finally be saved, without claiming to know (associated with Karl Barth’s refusal to rule it out, Hans Urs von Balthasar, Brad Jersak). <strong>Convinced universalism</strong> (apokatastasis): God will in the end restore all, through judgement, not around it (Gregory of Nyssa, Isaac the Syrian as usually read, George MacDonald, Thomas Talbott, Robin Parry, David Bentley Hart). Hart argues that if Christianity is coherent, “the universalist understanding of its message is the only one possible.”",
          "<strong>Conditional immortality</strong> (annihilationism): the unsaved are raised and judged, but since immortality is given only in Christ, they finally cease to exist, the “second death” (Edward Fudge, John Stott, John Wenham). <strong>The traditional view</strong>: eternal conscious punishment, the majority view in Western history (Augustine, Aquinas, the Reformers, much of evangelicalism), resting on texts like Matt 25:46, Mark 9:43-48 and Rev 14:11; 20:10. Many Orthodox Christians add that hell is experiencing God’s love as torment when one refuses it."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Scripture in context",
        "body": [
          "Scripture insists on one Saviour: “There is salvation in no one else” (Acts 4:12); “no one comes to the Father, except through me” (John 14:6). It also shows God at work beyond Israel’s borders: Melchizedek (Gen 14:18-20), Jethro, Rahab, Naaman, the Ninevites, the Magi. Peter says “in every nation he who fears him and works righteousness is acceptable to him” (Acts 10:35), yet still preaches Jesus to Cornelius (Acts 10:36-43). John says the true light “enlightens everyone” (John 1:9).",
          "On judgement, Jesus speaks soberly of “eternal punishment” (Matt 25:46). Scholars debate the words: <em>aiōnios</em> can mean “eternal” or “of the age to come”, and <em>kolasis</em> can carry the sense of correction, though many lexicons and interpreters disagree. Paul’s horizon is wide: God will “sum up all things in Christ” (Eph 1:10), reconcile “all things” (Col 1:20), and every knee will bow (Phil 2:10-11). Universalists read these as final restoration; others read them as universal acknowledgement, not universal salvation. Honest readers admit both kinds of texts are there."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The relational answer",
        "body": [
          "The relational gospel starts from who the Judge is: the Father has given all judgement to the Son (John 5:22), the one who died for his enemies. His judgement is real; it exposes and condemns everything that destroys his creatures. But it is the judgement of the Healer, aimed at setting right, not at endless retribution. Whatever is true of other faiths, anyone who is saved is saved by Jesus, and Jesus is better than our fears.",
          "So we honour the truth and goodness in other traditions (Acts 17:23, 28), and we still speak of Jesus, because he is not one way among many but the Way in person, the face of the Father. Relational-gospel voices differ. T.F. Torrance, for example, rejected universalism as a logical system, while also rejecting limited atonement; Kruger stresses inclusion and leaves the final outcome to God’s love; others, like Hart, argue for certain universal restoration. What they share is a confident hope rooted in Christ, not in the sincerity of religions."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A fair counter-view and a response",
        "body": [
          "The traditional case is weighty: Jesus spoke more about Gehenna than anyone else in the New Testament; the language of “eternal” for punishment parallels “eternal” for life (Matt 25:46); and the church’s majority voice for centuries held eternal punishment. Exclusivists add that inclusivism and universalism weaken the urgency of mission and can make the cross seem unnecessary. Conditionalists, in turn, argue that Scripture’s language of destruction and death (Matt 10:28; 2 Thess 1:9; Rom 6:23) fits final extinction better than endless torment.",
          "These views deserve respect; sincere, Bible-loving Christians hold each one. The relational response is to read the hard texts through the face of Jesus, to take God’s stated desire seriously (1 Tim 2:4; 2 Pet 3:9), and to say honestly where we hope rather than know. We do not need to be certain about every person’s destiny to be certain about God’s character."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "What to do",
        "body": [
          "1) Learn your neighbour’s faith from them, with respect, before you explain yours. 2) Look for what is true and good in it, and for the longing behind it. 3) Speak of Jesus as a gift, not as a weapon: “Can I tell you what I have found in him?” 4) Never use hell to frighten children or vulnerable people into faith.",
          "5) Hold your view of hell with humility; study the main views and the texts for yourself. 6) Leave final judgement to Christ, who alone knows hearts (1 Cor 4:5), and trust that “the Judge of all the earth” will do right (Gen 18:25). 7) Pray for all people, as Paul asks (1 Tim 2:1-4)."
        ]
      }
    ],
